{ A dead draw

RULE
King and Rook draw vs. King, Rook, a-pawn on the 7th (with its Rook trapped in front of it), and an h-pawn.

This is new, and should be added to your rapidly growing store of memorized basic positions. Black can’t win because the h-pawn isn’t able to deprive the white King of both critical squares (it can take away g2, but not h2). Thus: }
1... h4 2. Kh2 (2. Ra6+ Kf5 3. Ra5+ Ke4 4. Ra4+ Kd5 5. Ra5+ Kc4 6. Ra8 Kb3 7. Rb8+ Ka4 8. Ra8+ Kb5 9. Rb8+ Ka6 10. Ra8+ Kb7 11. Ra3 Kb6 12. Rb3+ Ka5 13. Ra3+ Kb4 14. Ra8) (2. Kf2 Rh1 3. Rxa2 Rh2+ { [%cal Gh2a2] }) (2. Ra5 h3+ { [%cal Bg2h3] } 3. Kxh3 Rh1+ { [%csl Ra1][%cal Gh1h3,Ra2a1] }) 2... h3 3. Ra8 (3. Kxh3?? Rh1+ $19 { [%cal Ga2a1] }) 3... Kf5 4. Ra7 Ke4 5. Ra8 Kd3 6. Ra7 Kc2 7. Ra8 Kb3 8. Rb8+ Kc3 9. Ra8 $10 { Black’s King can wanderthe Earth like Cain, but it can’t bother white’s King, nor can it help extricate its Rook from the prison on al. } 9... Kb3 10. Rb8+ Ka4 11. Ra8+ { [%csl Rh2] } 11... Kb5 12. Rb8+ Ka6 13. Ra8+ Kb7 { [%csl Ga2][%cal Ga8a2] } 14. Ra3 Kb6 15. Rb3+ Ka5 16. Ra3+ Kb4 17. Ra8 Kb3 18. Rb8+ *


