{ Black wins with accurate play

Since building block four was an easy win for White, it’s clear that the same holds true here if Black can win the f4-pawn. The winning method is simple and instructive. }
1... Kc7 { If Black wants to make contact with the f4-pawn, he must break through the 6th rank. } 2. Kh2 { [%csl Gb7][%cal Gc7b7] } (2. Ra3 Kc6) 2... Kb7 3. Ra3 { [%csl Ga3,Re4][%cal Gb7b5,Ga6d6,Ga5d5] } (3. Ra4 Kb6 4. Ra8 Kb5 5. Ra7 Kc4 6. Ra4+ (6. Ra5 Kd4 7. Ra4+ Ke3 8. Kg2 Ke2 9. Ra3 Kd2 10. Ra4 { [%csl Gc2,Ga8][%cal Gd2c2,Ga4a8] } 10... Ke3 11. Ra3+ Kxf4) 6... Kd3 7. Kg2 Ke3) (3. Rg6 Rh1+ { [%csl Ga1][%cal Ga2a1] }) 3... Kb6 4. Kg2 Kb5 5. Ra8 Kb4 6. Ra7 Kc3 7. Ra5 Kd3 { Of course, White has no fear of 8.Rxf5 } 8. Ra8 (8. Rxf5 Rb1 $19 { [%cal Ga2a1] }) 8... Ke4! { [%cal Ga8a4] } (8... Ke3?! 9. Ra4!? { Black wants White to move here! Black to move can’t force White to implode, so he would have to dance around a bit until this same position is reached with White to move } 9... Kd3 10. Kh2 (10. Ra8 Ke4 { transposes to our main line }) 10... Ke2 { [%cal Re2f2,Re2f3] } 11. Kg2 Ke3 $19 { reaching the diagrammed position with White to move and winning due to zugszwang, as explained in our mainline. }) 9. Ra4+ Ke3 { Zugzwang! White is forced to drop his pawn or move his King to a poor square. } { [%cal Ga4a3] } 10. Kh2 (10. Ra3+ Ke2 { [%cal Ga3a4] } 11. Ra4 Ke3 { [%cal Gg2h2] } 12. Kh2 Kf3 13. Kh3) 10... Kf3 { and White must give up the pawn } 11. Ra8 (11. Kh3?? Rh1#) 11... Kxf4 { we are back to building block four.
These six building block positions should give you enough Flowchart knowledge to make solving our original two “problems” possible. } *


