[Event "ENDGAMES FOR EXPERTS (2000-2199): Rook and Two Connected Passed Pawns vs. Rook and Pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/7Sq816Cs/3SCKR5C9"]
[Date "2024.11.11"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame  (1899- 2000 )"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR EXPERTS (2000-2199): Rook and Two Connected Passed Pawns vs. Rook a"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LS4yALPI/il0va5YZ"]
[FEN "8/6p1/6k1/1r6/3PR3/2P5/8/6K1 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "06:58:54"]

{ White wins.

Positions featuring two passed pawns vs. one enemy passed pawn are usually winning for the two pawns. Previous knowledge is important here, too, since many positions are won by following basic rules, and by exchanging a pair of pawns and creating a winning Rook and one vs. Rook position. }
1. Kf2 { The King MUST take part in this battle! } (1. c4 Rb4 { [%csl Gb4] } 2. c5 Rc4 { [%csl Gc4,Gc5,Gf5][%cal Gg6f5] } 3. Kf2 Kf5 4. Rh4 (4. Re5+ Kf4 { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gc4d4] } 5. Rd5 Ke4 (5... g5 6. Rxg5 Kxg5 7. Ke3 Kf5 8. Kd3 Rc1 9. Kd2 Ra1) 6. Re5+ Kxd4 { [%cal Gd4c5] }) 4... g5 { [%csl Gg5] }) 1... Kf5 2. Kf3 (2. Ke3 { [%cal Gb5b3] } 2... Rb3 3. Kd3 (3. Re5+ Kg4 4. Kd3) 3... Rxc3+ 4. Kxc3 Kxe4 { [%cal Bg7g1] }) 2... g5 { [%cal Gf3e3,Ge3d3] } 3. Re8 { White’s Rook prepares to place itself behind the enemy passed pawn as discussed in Part Six). } 3... Rb1 4. Rf8+ Ke6 5. Ke4 { White isn’t too concerned with black’s pawn. Instead, he continues to bring his own King up so it can support his two passers. } 5... Re1+ 6. Kd3 { [%csl Bc3,Bd4] } 6... Rd1+ { [%csl Gc3,Gd4] } (6... g4?! 7. Re8+ Kd5 8. Rxe1 $18) 7. Kc4 { [%csl Gc4,Gd8][%cal Gd4d5,Gd5d6] } 7... g4 { [%csl Gc4,Gd4] } 8. Kc5 { Optimizing the position of white’s King. He’s finally ready to start moving his own pawns forward. Please note: he will only do so after the King gets into a position to support them. } 8... g3 9. Rg8 { Placing the Rook behind black’s passed pawn and stopping it from dashing to g1. } 9... Rg1 10. c4 { At last! } 10... g2 11. d5+ Kd7 12. Rg7+ Ke8 { REMEMBER :
You can avoid Rook checks by keeping you King in front of your pawns ! } { [%csl Gc5][%cal Gg1c1,Gc1c5] } 13. Kd6 { White heads for a winning Rook and pawn vs. Rook endgame. Even stronger is 13.d6 : Kf8 18.Re4 Kf7 15.Kc6 Rel 16.d7, 1-0. However, the play after 13.Kd6 is more instructive. } (13. d6!? Kf8 14. Rg4 Kf7 15. Kc6 Rc1 16. d7 $18 { the play after 13.Kd6 is more instructive. }) 13... Rc1 { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gg7g2] } (13... Kf8 14. Rg4 Rc1 15. Rxg2 Rxc4 16. Kd7 $18 { would transpose into our main line. }) 14. Rg8+ { This check forces black’s King away from e8 and thus gives white’s King access to the d7-square. } 14... Kf7 15. Rxg2 Rxc4 16. Kd7 { White is heading for a Lucena Position (explored in Part Four). Black tries to resist, but his position is too far gone. } 16... Ra4 (16... Rd4 17. d6 Kf8 18. Kc7 Rc4+ 19. Kd8 Rd4 20. d7 Rc4 { [%cal Gd8e8,Gd8e7] } 21. Rf2+ { [%cal Gf8g7] } 21... Kg7 22. Ke7 (22. Rf5 Rc2 (22... Kg6 23. Rf3 Kg7 { [%csl Gd3][%cal Gf3d3] } 24. Rf2 Kg6 (24... Kh7 25. Rd2 { [%csl Gd2,Gd8][%cal Gd8e7,Ge7f6,Gf6g5,Gg5g4,Gg4g3,Gg3f2,Gf2e1,Gd7d8] }) 25. Rf8 Kg7 26. Re8 Kf7 27. Re3 Kf8 28. Rf3+ Kg7) 23. Ke7 Re2+ 24. Kd6 Rd2+ 25. Rd5 (25. Kc6 Rc2+ 26. Kd5 Rd2+ { [%csl Gd4][%cal Gf4d4] })) 22... Re4+ 23. Kd6) 17. Rf2+ { Trapping black’s King away from the action (see Part Four). } 17... Kg7 18. d6 Ra7+ { Poor Black doesn’t even have Checking Distance (explained in Part Six). } 19. Kc6 Ra6+ 20. Kc7 Ra7+ 21. Kb6 Rd7 22. Kc6 { 1-0 } 22... Rd8 23. Kc7 Ra8 24. d7 $18 Ra7+ 25. Kd8 Ra1 26. Kc7 (26. Rf4 { [%csl Gf4] } 26... Rd1 27. Ke7 Re1+ 28. Kd6 Rd1+ 29. Ke6 Re1+ 30. Kd5 Rd1+ 31. Rd4 Rxd4+ 32. Kxd4 Kf7 { [%csl Rd8][%cal Rd7d8] }) (26. Rc2 Kf7 27. Kc7 Ra7+ 28. Kd6 Ra6+ (28... Ra8 29. Rc8 Ra6+ 30. Kc5) 29. Rc6 Rxc6+ 30. Kxc6 Ke7 31. Kc7 { [%cal Gc7d8] }) (26. Rd2 Ra8+ 27. Kc7 Ra7+ 28. Kb6 Ra8 29. d8=Q Rxd8 30. Rxd8 Kf7 31. Kc7 Ke7 32. Rd5 { [%cal Gd5h5,Gh5h8,Gh8d8,Gd8d5] } 32... Ke6 33. Kc6 { [%cal Gc6d5] } 33... Ke7 34. Rd6 { [%csl Gd6][%cal Ge6h6,Gh6h8,Gh8d8,Gd8d6] } 34... Ke8 35. Rd7 { [%cal Gd7h7,Gh7h8,Gh8d8,Gd8d7] } 35... Kf8 { [%cal Gf8e7] } 36. Kd6 Ke8 37. Re7+ { [%csl Gd8][%cal Ge8d8] } (37. Ke6 { [%cal Ge8f8] } 37... Kf8 38. Re7 Kg8 39. Rf7 Kh8 40. Kf6 Kg8 41. Kg6 Kh8 42. Rf8#)) (26. Rf5 { [%csl Gf5] } 26... Kg6 27. Rd5 { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gd5d7] } 27... Kf7 28. Kc7 Rc1+ 29. Kb6 Rb1+ 30. Kc5 Rc1+ 31. Kb4 Rb1+ 32. Kc3 Rc1+ 33. Kb2) 26... Rc1+ 27. Kd8 Rd1 { [%cal Gf2f4] } 28. Rf4 Kg6 29. Kc7 Rc1+ 30. Kd6 Rd1+ 31. Kc6 Rc1+ 32. Kd5 Rd1+ 33. Rd4 { [%csl Bd4] } 33... Rxd4+ 34. Kxd4 *


