{ White to move
Black’s worst nightmare

This common endgame is also a draw if Black has prevented white’s pawns from eating up too much space (the space-gaining pawns would result in black’s King being pushed back and his pawns becoming vulnerable).

Here, Black is doomed because white’s pawns are far too strong. Usually this kind of debacle is caused by passive defensive play. } { [%csl Gd4,Ga8][%cal Gd7d4,Ga4a8] }
1. Kh5 { Preventing ...Kg6 when f5-f6 is played. } { [%csl Yg6][%cal Yh5g6,Rf5f6,Ra4a8] } 1... Rd8 { White threatened a back rank mate after f5-f6, so Black was compelled to prevent this with his Rook. } { [%cal Ga8g8] } 2. f6+ Kg8 { [%csl Gd8,Ga8][%cal Gd8d6,Ga4a8] } 3. Kh6 Rb8 { Black is helpless since any Rook move off the back rank allows mate by Ra8. } 4. Ra7 { Threatening 5.e6! fxe6 6.Rxh7 when Black should quietly resign. } { [%cal Re5e6,Ra7g7] } 4... Re8 { “T stopped it,” thinks Black. Alas, he’s wrong. } 5. e6! { 1-0 } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Gf7e6] } 5... Rxe6 (5... fxe6 6. Rg7+ (6. f7+ { [%cal Gf7e8,Gf7g8] }) 6... Kf8 7. Rxh7 $18 { [%cal Gg5g6,Gg6g7,Gg7g8] } 7... Rb8 8. Rh8+ { [%cal Gh8b8] }) (5... Rf8 6. Rxf7 Rxf7 7. exf7+ Kxf7 8. Kxh7) 6. Ra8+ $18 { UseFuL ADVICE :
Don't play passively and let the superior side's pawns board and eat up every bit of space! } *


