{ White wins thanks to the power of his pawns.

White has achieved a dominant pawn position and can now
force a win by mixing two plans:

- Moving the King and Rook out of the way and then playing for c4-c5-c6.

- mame Placing the Rook on the 7th rank, threatening to take advantage of the pinned c-pawn by d5-d6. }
1. Re6 { It’s important to move to the e-file since in some lines when White plays Kc6, a check along the 6th rank can be met by the white Rook returning to e6 (where it’s defended by the d-pawn). } 1... Rc1 { Forced, } (1... Rb2? { [%csl Gh7][%cal Gd5d6] } 2. Re7 { [%cal Rd5d6] } 2... Kb8 3. Kc6 $18) 2. Re8! { This forces the black Rook to step up to c2 or c3 when there won’t be sufficient Checking Distance (explained in Part Six) to bother the white King when it moves off of the c5-square. } 2... Rc2 { [%csl Gb7][%cal Ge8a8] } (2... Rg1? 3. Re7 { [%cal Rd5d6] } 3... Kb8 4. Kc6 Rg6+ 5. Re6 Rg8 6. c5 { Once again, the pawns roll Black off the board } 6... Rd8 (6... Rh8?? 7. Kd7 $18) 7. Re7 Rc8 8. Rh7 $18 { and Black loses the c-pawn. }) (2... a6? { Trying to exchange pawns, butit’s too late for this to help! } 3. bxa6+ Kxa6 4. Rb8 $18 { trapping the enemy King away from the action and when the threat of Rb4 followed by Kc6 is decisive. } { [%cal Rb8b4,Rc5c6] }) 3. Re1! { Preparing to pull the white King away from c5. Now the black Rook doesn’t have access to the Ist rank, which means it won’t be able to gain serious Checking Distance. } 3... Rc3 (3... Rh2? 4. Re7 $18) 4. Kd4 Rc2 5. Kd3 (5. c5? Rd2+ $10) 5... Rh2 (5... Rb2? 6. Kc3 $18) 6. c5 { Success! White’s pawns have achieved their dream positions. } { [%cal Gd3c4,Gc4b4,Gb4a5,Ge1e8] } 6... Rh3+ (6... Rb2?! { doesn’t fare any better } 7. Re7 Rxb5 8. Kc4 Rb1 9. d6 Rc1+ 10. Kd5 Rd1+ 11. Ke6 Re1+ 12. Kf7 Rf1+ 13. Ke8 $18) 7. Kd4 Rh4+ 8. Re4 Rh7 9. Re8 Rh4+ 10. Kc3 Rh5 (10... Rh3+ 11. Kb4 Rh4+ 12. Ka5 $18) 11. Kb4! (11. Rd8 a6 { is less forcing. }) 11... a5+! (11... Rxd5?? 12. c6+ Kb6 13. Rb8#) 12. Kxa5 Rxd5 13. c6+ Ka7 { [%csl Gc8][%cal Ge8c8,Gc8c7] } 14. Re7! { Giving black’s King a bit of room } { [%cal Ga7b8] } (14. Rc8?? Rxb5+! 15. Kxb5 { stalemate } { [%cal Gb5a6,Gc8a8] }) 14... Kb8 { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga5a6] } 15. Ka6 Rd8 { [%cal Gb5b6] } 16. b6! Rc8 (16... cxb6 17. c7+ { [%cal Gc7d8] }) 17. Rd7 (17. b7 Rd8 18. Kb5 Rd5+ { [%cal Ge7e8] }) 17... cxb6 { [%cal Gd7a7] } 18. Kxb6 { in easily (this position with Black having a passive Rook was examined in Part Four).
Clearly, the defender can’t allow his opponent to march his pawns down the board in this fashion. However, by strategically placing the black pawns so that any white advance leads to soothing exchanges, the game can usually be saved without too much difficulty. } (18. Rb7+ Ka8 19. c7 Rxc7 20. Rxc7 Kb8 21. Kxb6 Ka8 22. Rc8#) 18... Ka8 19. Rb7 { [%csl Gb7] } (19. c7 Rg8 20. Rd8+ { [%csl Gd8][%cal Gc7d8] }) 19... Rxc6+ *


