{ Black to move and save himself

If you imagine this coming from a four vs. three situation (chopped down to a three vs. two and finally two vs. one), it’s clear that White has made real progress. In fact, one can imagine Black panicking here and going down in multi-colored flames: look 1...Re8??

Fortunately, if you studiously learned the lessons in the order given, you'll be aware of the “Philidor gone bad” formula and, without hesitation, toss your Rook behind white’s (soon to be) passed f-pawn. }
1... Rf4! { This isn’t ideal since we’d prefer the Rook on f1 (Checking Distance). However, it turns out that this still allows Black to draw via his usual “Philidor gone bad” techniques. } (1... Re8?? 2. Kxf6 Kg8 3. Kg6 Rc8 4. f6 $18 { easy win thanks to the passive black Rook as seen in Part Four. }) 2. Kxf6 (2. Ra8+ Ke7 $10 { only helps Black. }) (2. Ra7 Rf1 $10 { ineffective, with a position explored earlier in Part Seven. I }) (2. Rxf6+ Kg8 3. Ra6 Rg4+ 4. Kf6 Rf4 { same position that occurs after 2.Kxf6. }) 2... Kg8! { Going to the Short Side of the board so, in somelines, black’s Rook can have Checking Distance ontheside. } 3. Ra8+ (3. Ra7 Rf1 { Nothing is changed }) (3. Ke6 Re4+ 4. Kf6 Rf4 { Nothing is changed }) (3. Kg6 Rg4+ 4. Kf6 Rf4 { also achieve nothing. }) 3... Kh7 4. Ke6 (4. Rf8 Ra4! { Here’s that Checking Distance I mentioned a moment ago } 5. Re8 Rf4! $10) 4... Kg7 { White can’t make progress. } *


