{ Where the presence of opposite colored Bishops gives the defender hope of drawing, same-colored Bishops usually guarantee the side with the extra pawn(s) serious winning chances.

here, White wins easily.

When up a pawn (with no other pawns remaining), the stronger side has winning chances if the pawn is advanced and if the defending King isn’t able to get in front of it. In general, a situation will often be reached where the defending Bishop is preventing the pawn from advancing (an immediate draw will result if it can sacrifice itself for the last pawn). The stronger side can only win if his own Bishop can block the diagonal of the defending Bishop. Diagram offers a simplistic example of this. }
1. Be5 Kg6 2. Bc7 { 1-0. Even if the weaker side’s Bishop is on the longer diagonal (d8-h4 instead of d8-a5, as in the following position) the game is still “in the bag.” } *


