[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "06:58:54"]

{ L. Centurini 1847
White wins

RULE :
The idea of a shortened defensive diagonal means that one's winning chances improve when the pawn is a bishop-pawn and knight pawn.

White wins by chasing the black Bishop off the h2-b8 diagonal and onto the pathetically short a7-b8 complex. Then a timely Bishop move/sacrifice along the g1-a7 diagonal forces the black Bishop off the queening square and allows the pawn to successfully promote. } { [%csl Rb8][%cal Gd8h4,Gh4f2,Gf2a7,Ga7b8,Rb8h2] }
1. Bh4 Kb5! { More resistance ! } (1... Bf4 2. Bf2 Bh2 3. Ba7 Bg3 4. Bb8 Bf2 5. Bf4 Ba7 { Black is now undone due to the lack of diagonal length. } 6. Be3! $18 { The seemed easy, but Black can improve and put up some annoying resistance with 1...Kb5! }) 2. Bf2 Ka6 { Suddenly Black has prevented Ba7. Can White still win? } 3. Bc5! { only good move, Getting ready to swing back to c7. Very
important since it stopped Black from moving his Bishop to d6, which in turn allows White to successfully swing his Bishop with tempo to the g1-a7 diagonal and avoid contact with black’s King.
It prevents ...Bd6 and forces the black Bishop to move to g3, f4, or e5. When White ultimately challenges that Bishop via Bh4, Bg5, or Bf6, white’s Bishop will then be out of reach of the enemy King (now on c6) when it moves to f2, e3, or d4. } (3. Be3? Bd6 4. Bg5 Kb5 5. Bd8 Kc6 6. Be7 Bh2 { and 7.BcS isn’t possible }) 3... Be5 { Black will rush his King back to c6, but he can’t doit yet since 3...Kb5 is met by 4.Ba7. } { [%csl Gc7][%cal Gc5e7,Ge7d8,Gd8c7] } (3... Kb5 4. Ba7 $18 { easy }) 4. Be7 Kb5 { Else White wins by Bd8 followed by Bc7. } (4... Kb6) 5. Bd8 Kc6 { It seems like Black has stopped White again, but now a nice maneuver shows that the game is, in fact, over. } 6. Bf6! Bf4 7. Bd4 { when Black can no longer keep white’s Bishop off of a7. } { [%csl Ga7][%cal Gd4a7] } 7... Bg3 8. Ba7 Bf4 9. Bb8 Be3 10. Bg3 Ba7 { [%csl Ra7] } 11. Bf2 $18 { Centurini, in the middle of the 19th century, postulated that a zugzwang isn’t possible if two free squares along the crucial diagonal remain open to the defending Bishop. If the defending Bishop hasless than two, then the stronger side will usually win (there are a couple of exceptions, but since we’re after concept and not brute memorization, we’ll let these pass by—if you wish to make an in-depth study of this kind of Bishop endgame, you can find the exceptions
in any detailed endgame tome). } *


