{ White wins. Two extra protected passed pawns usually assure the stronger side of the full point. }
1. d4+ { An important idea: pushing the d-pawn takes control of the dark squares (c5 and e5) while the Bishop keeps an eye on the light squares. This team control over the key squares forces the enemy King back.
White must be careful to avoid letting the enemy King create a blockade. This would occur after 1.e4?? Kd4 when the game is drawn since White can’t march his King to f3 and f4 due to ...Kxd3, while 2.Bf1 (preparing for the King
march to f4) runs head long into ...Bg6 followed by ...Bxe4-. } (1. e4?? Kd4 $10) 1... Kd6 2. e4 Bg6 3. Ke3 { It’s very important to support the pawns with the King! } 3... Bh7 4. e5+ (4. d5?? { losing control over the dark squares } 4... Ke5 $10 { create a blockade }) 4... Ke6 5. Kf4 Bc2 6. d5+ Kd7 7. Bh3+ Ke7 8. d6+ Kd8 9. e6 Ba4 10. Ke5 (10. e7+!? { nothing wrong. But I like the idea of making sure every piece participates in the execution. }) 10... Bb5 11. Kf6 Ba4 12. e7+ Ke8 13. Bg4 Bb5 14. d7+! (14. Bh5+ Kd7 15. e8=Q+ $18) 14... Bxd7 15. Bh5# { Pretty easy, as long as you don’t allow one of two things:
- Don’t let the defender give up his Bishop for both pawns.
- Don’t let the defender achieve a blockade by creating a hole (which the defender’s King would live on) on the opposite color of your Bishop. } *


[Event "ENDGAMES FOR EXPERTS (2000-2199): Blockade — Queen vs. Rook and Pawn"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/study/7Sq816Cs/3Dx9FX6f"]
