[Result "*"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/carbone144"]
[Variant "From Position"]
[ECO "?"]
[Opening "?"]
[StudyName "Endgame  (1899- 2000 )"]
[ChapterName "ENDGAMES FOR EXPERTS (2000-2199): Blockade — Queen vs. Rook and Pawn"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LS4yALPI/9w4K3S1c"]
[FEN "1Q6/3pk3/4r3/5K2/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2024.11.11"]
[UTCTime "06:58:54"]

{ Draw
In general, any pawn on the 2nd rank other than a rook-pawn will give the defender a draw unless the stronger side’s King has managed to break through to the 6th rank. A knight-pawn almost always draws.

Black has all the key bases covered:

- His Rook is stopping white’s King from penetrating to the 6th and/or 7th ranks.

- His Rook can mark time by going to two safe posts (c6 and
e6). A rook-pawn only allows the defender one safe post.

- White's Queen isn’t able to get behind the pawn and it will
never be able to chase black’s King away from e7, e8, or c7, c8. }
1. Qh8 Rc6 2. Qg7+ Kd8 $10 { White can’t improve his position.
Such Queen vs. pawn endgames can be very complicated and rule dependent (and these rules change as the pawn advances), and are far beyond anything a non-professional needs to study. Suffice it to say that if you keep in mind our
one example and its rules, you'll have something to grasp onto if you’re down material and this kind of defensive situation happens to arise. } *


