{ Black to move
Assess the position. }
1... hxg5 { Your past knowledge (Flowcharting a seemingly complex situation) should make it clear that White is winning if he avoids the many pitfalls that are lurking.
The key elements here are:
-White must not allow a Vancura Position.
-White must not allow a two pawn up position where his
a-pawn is on the 7th and his other pawnis a g-pawn.
- White must be aware that the win is easy in a two pawn up
position where the a-pawn is on the 7th and his other pawn is an f-pawn. } (1... fxg5 2. hxg5 { will transpose to our mainline. }) 2. hxg5 fxg5 3. f5! (3. fxg5?? Ra5! 4. Kd2 Rxg5 5. Kc3 Rg6 { White falling into a drawn Vancura Position } 6. Kd4 Kg7 7. Ke5 Rb6 (7... Rf6?? 8. Rg8+ Kxg8 9. Kxf6 $18) 8. Kd5 { White’s Rook is stuck to its pawn, and a7 allows ...Ra6, so White brings his King to the queenside. However, the Vancura holds firm. } 8... Rf6 9. Kc5 Kh7 10. Kb5 Rf5+ 11. Kc6 Rf6+ 12. Kb7 Rf7+ $10) 3... g4 (3... Kg7 4. a7! g4 5. f6+ Kh7 6. f7 $18) (3... Ra5 4. a7 { when the threat of f6 is decisive and 4...Rxf5 doesn’t help since 5.Rb8 will leave White with an extra Rook. }) 4. a7 { Freezing black’s King and Rook. } 4... g3 { Threatening to win by 5...g2. } { [%cal Rg3g2] } 5. Kf1! Kg7 { Nothing works, } (5... Rf2+ 6. Kg1 Rxf5 7. Rb8 { forces the win of black’s Rook (or creates a new Queen), }) (5... g2+ 6. Kg1 { leaves Black unable to deal with the threat of f6-f7-f8. }) 6. f6+! Kh7! (6... Kxf6 7. Rf8+ Kg7 8. a8=Q) (6... Kf7 7. Rh8 Rxa7 8. Rh7+ { [%cal Gh7a7] }) 7. Kg1! { Black must resign since he has no answer to the threat of f7 and f8=Q. } (7. f7 Rf2+ 8. Kg1 Rxf7 9. Rh8+ Kxh8 10. a8=Q+ { but why rush into a complex Queen vs. Rook position when the text move makes things easy? }) *


