[UTCTime "06:58:54"]

{ White to move
Another seemingly ungraspable position is really just a prelude to yet another Flowchart puzzle. See if you can ascertain (or guess!) what basic concept will appear on the board. }
1. e5! { Blasting open the h1-a8 diagonal.
Believe it or not, a King and pawn endgame featuring Fox in the Chicken Coup will soon appear!
Optically White seems a bit better: his Bishop has possibilities along the h1- a8 diagonal, he possesses more central space, black’s doubled c-pawns create a certain lack of flexibility, and black’s a-pawn is weak. Tactically though, things are even worse—the c6-pawn and the Knight are unprotected. White makes immediate use of these factors to either win a pawn or transition the game into an easily won King and pawn endgame. } 1... fxe5 (1... d5 2. c4! fxe5 3. cxd5 c5 4. d6! cxd6 5. Bd5+ Kh8 6. Bc6 Rd8 7. Rxd6 $16 { very unpleasant position for Black } 7... Nb8 8. Rxd8 Rxd8 9. Bb5 a6 10. Bc4 exf4 11. Rxf4 g6 12. Rf6 Kg7 13. Rb6 { and Black, whose pawns are weak and whose Knight is dominated, is completely lost. }) 2. Bxc6 { Not only threatening the Knight, but also pinning it to the e8-Rook } 2... Re7 3. Bxd7 { Why exchange this nice Bishop for black’s Knight? Because it forces the black Rook onto an unprotected square. } 3... Rxd7 4. fxe5 Rxf1+ 5. Kxf1 Rf7+? { This preventsthe loss of a pawn, but allows a resignable King and pawn endgame to arise. Instead, Black should have kept the Rooks on the board } (5... d5 6. c4 d4 7. Ke2 Kf7 8. Kd3 Ke6 9. Ke4 c5 10. Rf1 Rd8 { not allowing White to penetrate to f8 when Black can battle on (ideas like ...Rb8 followed by ...a5 and ...a4 are screaming to be tried). }) 6. Kg2 (6. Ke2?? Re7 { when Black is suddenly okay. }) 6... dxe5 7. Rd8+ { This final transition, taking the game into a King and pawn ending, is decisive since white’s queenside majority will ultimately leave him with a Fox in the Chicken Coup situation. } 7... Rf8 8. Rxf8+ Kxf8 9. Kf3 Ke7 10. Ke4 Kd6 11. b4! { A very important move which leaves white’s queenside pawns free to roam. } (11. c4? c5 12. a3 a5 $10 { with a situation where two black pawns are stopping three white pawns. }) 11... a6 12. a4 g6 13. c4 a5 (13... c6 14. b5 axb5 15. axb5 cxb5 16. cxb5 Kc5 17. Kxe5 Kxb5 18. Kf6 $18 { is a classic Fox in the Chicken Coup. }) (13... Ke6 14. b5 a5 15. c5 $18) 14. bxa5 Kc5 15. Kxe5 { 1-0 Tarrasch - Hollaender, Nuremberg 1892 } *


