{ White to move.
Can White draw ? }
1. Kh4 { The defender is usually happy to trade off pawns, and in this case a draw can be achieved if he can exchange his three remaining pawns for black’s two. } (1. Kf6 { In the actual game, White lost quickly } 1... Kd8 2. Kf7 Bf1 3. e6 Bc4 4. Kg7 Ke7 5. Kxh7 Kxe6 6. Kg6 Ke5 7. Kg5 Be6 { 0-1 Karklins - Gufeld, American Open 1999 }) 1... Kd7 2. f3 h5 3. f4 (3. fxg4?? Bxg4 $19) 3... Ke6 4. Kxh5 (4. Kg5 { Also adequate } 4... Bg2 5. Kxh5 Kf5 6. e6 Bc6 7. Kh4 Kxf4 8. e7 Kf3 9. Kg5 Bd7 10. Kh4 $10 { positional draw: Black can’t improvehis position } 10... Kf4 $10 { leads to the stalemate in our mainline, } (10... Ke4 { allow White to win black’s final pawn. }) (10... Kg2 { allow White to win black’s final pawn. })) 4... Kf5 5. Kh4 Kxf4 6. e6 Bg2 7. e7 Bc6 8. h3 g3 9. e8=Q Bxe8 $10 { stalemate } *


